Downloaded 2026-09-30 from https://the.earth.li/~sgtatham/putty/latest/ (the host
that https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/putty/latest.html links to):

  putty-0.85.tar.gz       3023114 bytes
  putty-0.85.tar.gz.gpg   438 bytes (detached signature)
  sha256sums              16416 bytes

Verification - run ./verify.sh to repeat it (exit status 0 means all of this holds):

  * SHA-256 of putty-0.85.tar.gz is
      13fd4db2936d03b73812a7bcc2a658e4dd29cc776a56c3670a7fc6f1a0ee8af8
    which matches the published sha256sums.
  * The tarball's signature is a good one by "PuTTY Releases
    <putty@projects.tartarus.org>", the Release Key (2026), fingerprint
      1A07 DAC0 F220 CF12 248E A719 5C67 D0F8 8BC3 E5B5
  * That key is certified by the PuTTY Master Key (2026), fingerprint
      371C 662B 855F 318F F63A D091 D732 471F 094D 5FB5
  * Both fingerprints are the ones printed on
    https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/putty/keys.html, and the master
    one is also compiled into the source (PGP_MASTER_KEY_FP in putty.h).

What this does not prove: the master key's fingerprint was cross-checked between
the official site and this tarball's own source, but not against an independent,
previously trusted copy of PuTTY.

keys/ holds the two public keys, fetched on 2026-10-01 from
https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/putty/keys/ .  Nothing is fetched
from putty.org: it is NOT the PuTTY project's website (see FAQ A.9.1 in the
PuTTY documentation).  verify.sh imports the keys into a throw-away keyring, so
no one's real GnuPG setup is touched.

putty-0.85/ is the unpacked tree (`tar xzf putty-0.85.tar.gz` in this
directory).  It is not tracked by git; the tarball is the source of truth.
